# cisco-bash-config

Bash scripts to automate the configuration of Cisco devices in network environments.

## System Requirements

- **Operating System:** Debian-based distribution (Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint, etc.)
- **Permissions:** No superuser (`root`) privileges required. All scripts are designed to run as a standard user.

## Project Structure

```
Cisco_Deployment_Learning/
├── .env                        # Environment variables (credentials, global settings)
├── .README                     # This file
├── inventory.conf              # Network device inventory
├── check_deps.sh               # Dependency checker script
├── deploy.sh                   # Main deployment script
├── lib/                        # Shared library scripts
│   ├── log.sh                  # Logging utilities
│   ├── render.sh               # Template rendering engine
│   └── ssh.sh                  # SSH connection helpers
├── logs/                       # Execution logs output directory
├── templates/                  # Cisco IOS configuration templates
│   ├── router_base.tpl         # Base router configuration template
│   ├── switch_l2_base.tpl      # Layer 2 switch base template
│   └── switch_l3_base.tpl      # Layer 3 switch base template
└── vars/                       # Per-device configuration variables
    ├── SW-DIST1.conf           # Variables for SW-DIST1
    └── SW-DIST2.conf           # Variables for SW-DIST2
```

## Before You Begin

> ⚠️ **Carefully review the following files before running any script:**

### `inventory.conf`

Defines the network devices that configurations will be applied to. Make sure that:

- Device IPs or hostnames are correct and reachable.
- Access credentials or methods (SSH key, username) are properly configured.
- Device groups are correctly defined for your environment.

### `vars/` Directory

Contains the variables that parameterize the configuration templates. Review each `.conf` file and verify that:

- VLAN IDs, interface names, IP ranges, and protocol settings (HSRP, STP, VTP, etc.) match your actual infrastructure.
- There are no naming conflicts between device-specific variable files.
- Test environment variables do not accidentally overwrite production settings.

### `templates/` Directory

Contains IOS/IOS-XE command templates that are rendered and pushed to devices. Verify that:

- Configuration blocks (interfaces, routing, ACLs, etc.) are correct for the target device model and IOS version.
- All variables referenced in templates (`{{ variable }}`) have a corresponding entry in `vars/`.
- Templates do not include unintended destructive commands (e.g., `no shutdown` on unintended interfaces, VLAN deletions, etc.).

### `.env` File

Contains global environment variables such as credentials and connection defaults. Ensure that:

- Sensitive values are not committed to version control (`.env` should be in `.gitignore`).
- All required variables are populated before running `deploy.sh`.

## Usage

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/usuario/cisco-bash-config.git
cd Cisco_Deployment_Learning

# Check dependencies
bash check_deps.sh

# Review and edit the inventory
nano inventory.conf

# Review device variables
nano vars/SW-DIST1.conf

# Run the deployment script
bash deploy.sh
```

> `sudo` is not used at any step. If any script prompts for a root password, stop execution and open an issue.

